{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgYymnyguEqzKWPwZfkn9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The paper is finished: `paper_draft/main.pdf` is 9 pages and compiles with no errors, undefined references or overfull boxes. While checking the report against the raw generations I found a scoring bug, and it needs your decision (see below). I reviewed the paper against the language, formatting and structure checklists, and looked at a contact sheet of all pages plus page 2 up close. Nothing is committed.\n\n## Scoring bug in `P_ahoy`\n`REPORT.md` says \"No systematic false negatives were found\", but that's wrong. The `P_ahoy` check only strips a title on its own line, so a compliant `<<Title>> Ahoy, matey!` counts as a failure. A corrected check changes 518 of 18,232 scores (509 become passes, 9 become failures), mostly for Qwen2.5-3B and Llama-3.2-1B. All other verifiers held up.\n\n| Result | Report | Corrected |\n|---|---|---|\n| F_title → P_ahoy interference | −12.5 pp | **−3.1 pp (mostly an artifact)** |\n| Position effect, all models | +1.7 pp | +1.4 pp (p = .009) |\n| Position effect, Qwen2.5-3B | +8.2 pp | +6.5 pp (p = .035) |\n| All instructions satisfied, k=5 | 37.0% | 38.2% |\n| Repetition gain | +3.3 pp | +3.5 pp |\n\nThe main conclusions don't change.\n\n**How the paper handles it:** the tables keep the report's numbers so they match `REPORT.md`. The verifier issue is disclosed in Methodology, and a dedicated sensitivity subsection and table give the corrected values. The artifact is no longer counted as evidence of interference, though the E2 heatmap still shows the −12 cell, with a note in its caption.\n\nThe rerun is in the new `src/sensitivity_ahoy.py`, with output in `results/analysis/sensitivity_ahoy/`. The original results, `src/instructions.py` and `REPORT.md` are unchanged, so **`REPORT.md` still contains the incorrect claim.**\n\n## Other differences from the report\n- **Slope range:** the report's \"2–4 pp per added instruction\" is now \"about 1.5–4 pp\", because Qwen3-8B is −1.47.\n- **Rounding:** \"93%\" at k≥5 is now 92%, and the weakest models' upper bound of 31% is now 30%.\n- **Model size:** the paper now states that the logit-scale size trend is not significant (p = .09). The report's \"at least as steeply\" reads more firmly than that.\n- **Multiple-testing correction:** E2's Holm correction runs across 32 group-level cells, not across models as the report says.\n- **Interference varies by model:** the −30 pp bullets → closing phrase effect ranges from −69 to 0 across models. The no-comma → closing phrase effect comes from only two Llama models. The report's averages hide both, so the paper states them.\n- **Added from the raw data:** a per-instruction table, a truncation sensitivity figure (at most 0.5 pp on the mean), and the P_matey → P_name pair.\n- **Dropped figure:** the H5 figure had overlapping axis labels, and its table has all the same data plus more.\n\n## Citations\n- **Harada et al.:** the report's \"Curse of Instructions\" title couldn't be confirmed from a primary source, so I cited the paper that does introduce ManyIFEval, \"When Instructions Multiply\" (Findings of EMNLP 2025).\n- **Lost in the Middle:** Crossref and arXiv confirm TACL 2024, and arXiv gives the authors, so those details are correct in the bibliography.\n- **IFEval and ComplexBench:** these are cited as arXiv preprints.\n- **Literature basis:** no literature review stage ran, and the paper says so.\n\n## Formatting\n- **Style file:** `[final]` alone leaves the NeurIPS footer's track name undefined, so I defined it as blank. This also means the paper doesn't claim to be a NeurIPS publication.\n- **Examples:** `paper_examples/` holds only a README, so the formatting follows the style guide alone.\n- **Author:** `.neurico/idea.yaml` doesn't exist, so the author is `NeuriCo` as you specified.\n\n## Decision for you\nShould the corrected numbers become the primary results? I kept the report's numbers so the paper stays traceable to it, as you required, but a final submission should probably use the corrected ones. That would mean replacing the numbers in `scores.csv`, regenerating the tables and updating `REPORT.md`; the changes are mechanical.\n\nSources:\n- [When Instructions Multiply (arXiv)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.21051)\n- [When Instructions Multiply (ACL Anthology)](https://preview.aclanthology.org/new-sigs/2025.findings-emnlp.896/)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1422,"cache_read_input_tokens":155405,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1422},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d14609fb-cedd-473d-b4a3-21afe836ae86","uuid":"cb134e13-5ed1-4310-86fb-c65b144024f0","timestamp":"2026-10-30T19:33:03.951Z","request_id":"req_011CgYymnkY5MuqZ21XMZK8Q"}
